\caption{Downstream impact of DIF-based item filtering on model rankings. Despite $\rho \approx 0.997$ overall, individual model families show systematic rank shifts when evaluated on stable items only.}
\label{app:fig_downstream}
\end{figure}

\section{Cross-Benchmark Comparison}
\label{app:gsm8k_details}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-benchmark comparison of DIF analysis on MMLU and GSM8K. Both benchmarks exhibit temporal item instability with validated semi-synthetic detection power. Standard and external matching use different exploitation rates ($p$) because each benchmark's optimal operating point differs; external matching results are compared at $p = 0.5$ for both. Subscript $\pm$ values denote standard deviations across 5 random injection seeds.}
\label{tab:gsm8k_comparison}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Metric & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
Items & 12,508 & 1,319 \\
Models & 5,227 & 6,702 \\
Temporal C\% & 31.3\% & 18.9\% \\
Sanity C\% & 0.18\% & 0.3\% \\
$\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.7$, standard) & -- & $+0.818_{\pm.006}$ \\
$\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$, external) & $+0.508_{\pm.027}$ & $+0.514_{\pm.028}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

A power analysis using empirical MMLU IRT parameters confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80, FPR of 0.036--0.042, and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$. Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold.

\subsection{Temporal Comparability: 31\% Item Instability}
\label{sec:temporal}

We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$). Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 items (31.32\% $\to$ 31.30\%), because the dual ETS threshold on effect size and significance already provides implicit multiple-testing protection.

Three controls confirm this reflects genuine temporal change. A random split of the 2023 cohort yields only 0.18\% ETS~C items (sampling noise), and an ability-quintile comparison within the same temporal cohort yields 2.9\% (ability differences alone). A within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3) produces 61.3\% ETS~C items, elevated by large ability gaps that confound the DIF signal~\citep{holland1988mh}. The contrast between 2.9\% (ability-controlled) and 61.3\% (ability-confounded) brackets the temporal rate of 31.3\%.

Across all 57 MMLU subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$); within-subject matching on 5 subjects yields 38.1\% as a robustness check (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). Item-level logistic regression shows domain accuracy improvement explains only 2.4 percentage points of 31.3\% C\% (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); 92.4\% of instability is item-specific. The conclusion is robust across thresholds: C\% $= 48.9\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.0$, $18.3\%$ at $\geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{DIF landscape across cohort definitions. ETS~C denotes the percentage of items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$. FPR is measured against Li et al.\ web-overlap labels on the ``clean'' (non-overlapping) subset. Enrichment is the ratio of observed to expected overlap between DIF flags and web-overlap labels.}
\label{tab:temporal_landscape}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Cohort Definition & $N_{\mathrm{ref}}$ / $N_{\mathrm{focal}}$ & ETS~C (\%) & FPR (Li clean) & Enrichment \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024) & 1,080 / 807 & 31.3 & 0.318 & 0.96 \\
Ability quintile (Q1--2 vs.\ Q4--5) & ${\sim}$2,600 / ${\sim}$2,600 & 2.9 & 0.027 & 1.19 \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ 3) & 161 / 308 & 61.3 & -- & -- \\
Random split (sanity) & 540 / 540 & 0.18 & -- & -- \\
Within-subject (avg, 5 subjects) & \textit{varies} & 38.1 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

The enrichment ratio of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} is 0.96 (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}), indistinguishable from chance. This null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$, ${+}0.674$ at $p = 1.0$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). An item can be globally contaminated yet function identically if all cohorts have comparable access. The Li et al.\ labels are the wrong ground truth for a differential method: the near-chance enrichment is expected, not evidence of failure.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF detects differential contamination when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

Three structural controls fail to reduce temporal C\% below 31\%: matched-ability quintiles, IRT $\theta$-conditioning ($r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$, FPR $= 0.312$), and within-subject DIF (C\% $= 38.1\%$, exceeding the 25\% bifactor viability threshold). Logistic regression DIF confirms 92.1\% concordance with MH and reveals additional non-uniform DIF (24.8\% at $|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$), making the 31\% estimate conservative. Graduated de-duplication ($K{=}10$: 22.7\% vs.\ random 26.8\%; $K{=}20$: 29.1\% vs.\ 30.9\%) confirms family structure does not inflate C\%. Ability-matched within-family analysis reduces Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3 C\% from 61.3\% (unmatched) to $28.3\% \pm 1.6\%$ (Cohen's $d = -0.99$, overlap $= 0.18$ vs.\ $d = -0.17$, overlap $= 0.66$ for temporal cohorts), indicating ability confounding explains roughly half; the residual 28.3\% is consistent with the temporal rate of 31.3\%. Full details in \Cref{app:structural}.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Framework Validation}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

The critical methodological finding is that the choice of matching strategy determines validity. Cross-subject matching computes the conditioning variable from subjects \emph{other than} the one being tested, achieving $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU subjects while four of five produce $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$. Within-subject matching inflates FPR by ${+}0.186$ on average because the matching variable absorbs part of the injection signal. We adopt cross-subject matching as the standard protocol (\Cref{app:fig_matching}).

\subsection{Downstream Impact}
\label{sec:downstream}

Spearman correlation between full and stable-item rankings is $\rho \approx 0.997$: aggregate model ordering is preserved despite 31\% item instability. DIF-flagged items nonetheless carry more discriminative signal than average ($z = -8$ to $-49$ vs.\ random removal across 21 conditions), though a circularity ablation shows DIF-based removal is the \emph{least} disruptive among non-random strategies (Jaccard overlap with high-discrimination items is only 0.12). Individual model families show distinctive DIF profiles: Llama-3 derivatives gain a median of ${+}53$ rank positions (mean ${+}121$), while Mistral-based models lose a median of $35$ (mean $-54$); individual extremes reach ${+}793$ and $-542$ (\Cref{app:fig_downstream}). Overall, 25\% of models shift by $|\Delta\mathrm{Rank}| \geq 100$. The DIF instability pattern extends beyond MMLU; \Cref{sec:gsm8k} presents a full replication on GSM8K.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

Replication on GSM8K (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models) yields temporal C\% $= 18.9\%$ (sanity check: 0.3\%), with semi-synthetic $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.514$ at $p = 0.5$ under external matching (\Cref{app:gsm8k_details}). As an exploratory analysis, the GSM1K held-out set~\citep{zhang2024gsm1k} provides independent contamination validation: for $N = 33$ models, the correlation between contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage is $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$; partial $r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$ after controlling for accuracy). This \emph{negative} correlation contradicts the hypothesis that DIF items benefit contaminated models, and instead suggests that DIF captures genuine capability change distinct from memorization (\Cref{fig:gsm1k}). The result is suggestive at $N = 33$ and warrants replication.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_5_gsm1k_dif_correlation.pdf}
\caption{GSM1K contamination gap vs.\ DIF ranking advantage ($N = 33$). The negative correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$) indicates that models with larger contamination gaps benefit less from DIF-flagged items.}
\label{fig:gsm1k}
\end{figure}

